## The Rundown: A Week of Missteps
As of this week, the tally of recalls has reached a hair-raising 759 this year, with Class I recalls--our most severe--making up a hefty 27%. July started with a bang, recording 11 recalls already. Utz Quality Foods, LLC, a familiar name on our recall radar, struggled with Salmonella contamination in their chips, while Spring & Mulberry's high-end chocolates were also pulled due to similar issues.

## The Repeat Offenders
Spring & Mulberry Inc. continues to set the wrong kind of records with their second recall this month, adding to an already dismal performance of 11 recalls this year alone. For boutique brands attempting to carve out a niche, this level of instability tarnishes trust quickly.

## The Allergen Ambush
Boichik Bagels, Inc. failed to disclose sesame seeds in their Pumpernickel Mix Bagels. For consumers with sesame allergies, this could spell disaster. Undeclared allergens remain a predominant cause behind recalls, accounting for a significant percentage of total incidents this year.

## What This Means For Consumers
Sadly, these recalls indicate glaring lapses in manufacturing practices. We urge consumers to pay close attention to recall announcements--especially if you or your loved ones have food sensitivities or strict dietary needs. For now, think twice before grabbing that bag of chips or indulging in specialty chocolates from certain brands.
